    "question": "Sienna gave Bailey half of her suckers. Jen ate half and gave the rest to Molly. Molly ate 2 and gave the rest to Harmony. Harmony kept 3 and passed the remainder to Taylor. Taylor ate one and gave the last 5 to Callie. How many suckers did Jen eat?",
    "gold": "11",
    "answer": "Taylor received 5 + 1 = <<5+1=6>>6 suckers.\nHarmony was given 3 + 6 = <<3+6=9>>9 suckers.\nMolly received 9 + 2 = <<9+2=11>>11 suckers from Jen.\nJen originally had 11 * 2 = <<11*2=22>>22 suckers.\nJen ate 22 – 11 = <<22-11=11>>11 suckers.\n#### 11"
  },
  "generation": {
    "text": "Let's denote the total number of suckers Sienna originally had as \\( x \\).\n\n1. Sienna gave Bailey half of her suckers. So, Bailey received \\( \\frac{x}{2} \\) suckers.\n2. Jen ate half of the suckers she received from Sienna and gave the rest to Molly. So, Jen ate \\( \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\frac{x}{2} = \\frac{x}{4} \\) suckers and gave the rest to Molly. The number of suckers Jen gave to Molly is \\( \\frac{x}{2} - \\frac{x}{4} = \\frac{x}{4} \\).\n3. Molly ate 2 suckers and gave the rest to Harmony. So, Molly gave \\( \\frac{x}{4} - 2 \\) suckers to Harmony. The number of suckers Molly gave to Harmony is \\( \\frac{x}{4} - 2 \\).\n4. Harmony kept 3 suckers and passed the remainder to Taylor. So, Harmony gave \\( \\frac{x}{4} - 2 - 3 = \\frac{x}{4} - 5 \\) suckers to Taylor. The number of suckers Harmony gave to Taylor is \\( \\frac{x}{4} - 5 \\).\n5. Taylor ate one sucker and gave the last 5 suckers to Callie. So, Taylor gave \\( \\frac{x}{4} - 5 - 1 = \\frac{x}{4} - 6 \\) suckers to Callie. The number of suckers Taylor gave to Callie is \\( \\frac{x}{4} - 6 \\).\n\nSince Taylor gave the last 5 suckers to Callie, we have:\n\\[ \\frac{x}{4} - 6 = 5 \\]\nSolving for \\( x \\):\n\\[ \\frac{x}{4} = 11 \\]\n\\[ x = 44 \\]\n\nNow, we can find out how many suckers Jen ate. Jen ate \\( \\frac{x}{4} = \\frac{44}{4} = 11 \\) suckers.\n\nTherefore, the number of suckers Jen ate is \\(\\boxed{11}\\).",
